Follow-up to bf16_matmul_ffn_real_shape_npu.log's two "not tested this session"
items: does `--c-col-maj 1` dodge the C-output byte-stride limit (limit 1) and
recover throughput, and does Mistral-7B's friendlier d_ff=14336 factorization
avoid the m=16 tile-size compromise Llama-2-7B's d_ff=11008 forced? Both
tried. Neither answer is the naive guess.
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), 2026-09-07, mlir-aie v1.4.2, ironenv
(NPU) + resnet_env (CPU baseline, torch 2.4.1+cpu).

PART 1: --c-col-maj 1 -- dodges limit 1, but doesn't help
===========================================================
`--c-col-maj 1` stores C column-major, which moves the C-output tap's
outer-repeat stride from depending on N (row-major: m*4*N*itemsize) to
depending on M instead. Since M=2048 << N=11008, this should relax limit 1
enough to use the default m=64 tile. Tested at N=11008 (Llama-2-7B shape):

  python whole_array.py --dev npu --dtype_in bf16 --dtype_out f32 \
      -M 2048 -K 4096 -N 11008 --c-col-maj 1 --n-aie-cols 4 \
      --iters 5 --warmup 2

Compiles and PASSes at the *default* m=64/n=32 -- confirms the byte-stride
limit is dodged. **But throughput is 811.69 GFLOPS, WORSE than the row-major
m=16 workaround's 909.97 GFLOPS.** Tried to push it higher by growing the
tile further (col-major relieves limit 1's pressure, so the natural next
move is a bigger m or n): both directions hit a NEW, fourth limit --
**AIE2 L1 (core-local) total tile memory, ~64 KiB, shared by the double-
buffered A/B/C tiles**:

  -n 64  (m=64 default): "allocated buffers exceeded available memory" --
    C_L1L2 buffers alone are 2x16384 bytes at m=64/n=64/f32, plus A/B
    buffers, over budget.
  -m 128 (n=32 default): same error, A_L2L1 buffers now the big ones.

So `--c-col-maj 1` trades limit 1 (DMA byte-stride) for limit 4 (L1 tile
memory) at a WORSE operating point for this shape -- not a useful lever here.
Verdict: the row-major m=16 workaround (bf16_matmul_ffn_real_shape_npu.log)
remains the better of the two available compile-clean options for N=11008.

PART 2: Mistral-7B's d_ff=14336 -- the factorization DOES matter, just not
the way first guessed
========================================================================
Llama-2-7B's d_ff=11008 = 2^8 * 43 forces n=64 as the *only* tile-n value
that survives all three DMA/BD limits (bf16_matmul_ffn_real_shape_npu.log),
because 2752 = 11008/4's only divisors that are multiples of 16 are
{16, 32, 64, 688, 1376, 2752} -- a large gap from 64 straight to 688 (688
blows the B-input word-length limit). The hypothesis going in: Mistral-7B's
d_ff=14336 = 2^11 * 7 is a "friendlier" factorization and might avoid the
whole compromise. **Wrong reason, right instinct: it doesn't relax limit 1
(m is still capped at 16, since that limit scales with N alone, independent
of N's factorization), but it DOES open up intermediate n values 11008
couldn't reach** -- 3584 = 14336/4's divisors that are multiples of 16
include 64, 112, **128**, 224, 256, 448, ... -- a much finer ladder.

  # m=16 forced exactly as with N=11008 (same limit-1 math); n swept:
  python whole_array.py --dev npu --dtype_in bf16 --dtype_out f32 \
      -M 2048 -K 4096 -N 14336 -m 16 -n 64  --n-aie-cols 4 --iters 10 --warmup 3
  python whole_array.py --dev npu --dtype_in bf16 --dtype_out f32 \
      -M 2048 -K 4096 -N 14336 -m 16 -n 128 --n-aie-cols 4 --iters 10 --warmup 3
  python whole_array.py --dev npu --dtype_in bf16 --dtype_out f32 \
      -M 2048 -K 4096 -N 14336 -m 16 -n 448 --n-aie-cols 4 --iters 10 --warmup 3

  n=64:  835.63 GFLOPS, PASS
  n=128: 1454.37 GFLOPS, PASS  -- 74% higher than n=64, from doubling n alone
  n=448: FAILS -- L1 tile memory exceeded (B buffers alone: 2x57344 bytes)

n=128 is the practical ceiling: n=224's B+C buffers alone (57344+28672=86016
bytes) already exceed the ~64 KiB L1 budget before A/stack are even added, so
224 was not run (the arithmetic settles it, matching the n=448 measured
failure's own numbers closely enough not to need a third data point).

Down-projection (K=14336, N=4096 -- unconstrained, same as the N=11008 case):
  python whole_array.py --dev npu --dtype_in bf16 --dtype_out f32 \
      -M 2048 -K 14336 -N 4096 --n-aie-cols 4 --iters 10 --warmup 3
  NPU time     (avg/min/max us): 141406.9 / 139905.5 / 144286.1
  End-to-end   (avg/min/max us): 142314.7 / 140906.3 / 145210.7
  NPU GFLOPS                    : 1700.89
  PASS!

CPU baseline (torch bf16, same one-off harness, Mistral shape):
  CPU FFN pipeline (Mistral-7B shape), M=2048 K=4096 N=14336 (d_ff)
    mean 352.978 ms, min 309.520 ms, max 408.163 ms over 10 iters
    effective GFLOPS: 1362.8
  CPU GELU alone on (2048,14336) bf16: mean 3.155 ms, min 2.845 ms

MISTRAL PIPELINE, BEST NPU CONFIG (m=16, n=128 up / default down)
=====================================================================
  NPU pipeline = up End-to-end (166.298 ms) + CPU GELU (3.155 ms)
               + down End-to-end (142.315 ms) = 311.768 ms
  CPU pipeline = 352.978 ms

  total FLOPs (both matmuls) = 2*(2*2048*4096*14336) = 481.04 GFLOP

      pipeline                             NPU GFLOPS   CPU GFLOPS   ratio    winner
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Mistral-7B (d_ff=14336) up->gelu->down      1542.9       1362.8     1.13x    NPU

THE FINDING
===========
**Two real 7B models' actual FFN shapes give opposite verdicts on the same
hardware, same toolchain, same limits.** Llama-2-7B (d_ff=11008): CPU wins
1.10x (bf16_matmul_ffn_real_shape_npu.log). Mistral-7B (d_ff=14336): **NPU
wins 1.13x**, this log. Both hit the identical limit-1 constraint forcing
m=16 for the up-projection -- the difference is entirely in how much n-tile
headroom each d_ff's factorization leaves under limits 2-4. 11008's stray
large prime (43) leaves none (n stuck at 64); 14336's 2^11*7 leaves room up
to n=128 (2x the throughput at m=16 alone).

**This sharpens, not just extends, bf16_matmul_ffn_real_shape_npu.log's
bottom line.** It is not "square wins, real shapes lose" -- Mistral's real,
non-square shape wins. It is not "d_ff must be small" -- Mistral's d_ff
(14336) is larger than Llama's (11008) and wins anyway. The actual
determinant this project can now name precisely: **whether d_ff's
factorization admits an n-tile large enough (ideally >=128 at this K, dtype,
and L1 budget) once m is forced down by the fixed ~4 MiB C-output byte-stride
cap.** That is a property of the specific integer, not of "real-world-ness"
in general -- a fact a model architect choosing d_ff for other reasons would
have no way to know without a check exactly like this one.

**Not tested:** whether a hand-tuned `k` (currently fixed at the default 64)
trades against `n` inside the same L1 budget in a way that recovers more
throughput at N=11008; `int8`/`int16` at either shape (a narrower dtype_out
would relax limit 1 further, per bf16_matmul_ffn_real_shape_npu.log's
byte-stride formula, but changes precision semantics and needs its own
correctness check); GPT-family d_ff values with other factorizations.
